+### 3.9 The idea action (`ui/idea.js`; decision of 2026-09-15)
+
+Every page of one repo carries one action that captures a line and opens a shaping session with the page's entity in the context pack, so an idea can be shaped the moment it strikes without leaving the page. The trigger is the header button of §3.1; on the workspace page it is the button on each repo tile (§2 Level 0). Clicking it opens `.idea`, a `role="dialog"` popover under the trigger on a desktop (right-aligned so it never leaves the viewport, flipped above the anchor when there is no room below) and a full-width strip under the header on a phone, holding: one sentence saying where the idea will land ("Into `reggie`, from the file `packages/reggie/src/serve.ts`." — per level the folder, the file, the symbol in its file, the task, or "as a repo-wide idea" on the overview, the board, the services, flow, people and time pages); one input; a main button reading "Capture and shape in Claude Code" (the remembered tool, `reggie.launch.tool`) with a caret naming both tools; an error line; and a result area. Focus goes to the input on open and back to the trigger on close; Cmd or Ctrl and Enter submits; Escape closes the tool menu first, then the popover; leaving the page closes it.
+
+The origin a page posts (`originFor(route)`): area → `path` (the folder), file → `path`, symbol → `path` (the file half of the id) and `symbol`, task → `task`, every other repo-level page nothing, the workspace nothing (the trigger is hidden there). On submit the form disables itself (input, main button, caret; a second click changes nothing) and posts `/api/capture` with the text and the origin; a refusal shows the server's sentence on the error line and re-enables the form. On 200 it toasts "Captured as `slug`", clears the input, invalidates the cache and redraws the level, puts a link to the new task first in the result area, and hands `[slug]` and the origin's path to the board's launcher in `discuss` mode (the server resolves the goal: a fresh slug is shaped). The captured text never reaches the launch: that body holds the slug, the tool, the mode and the path. The description (`GET /api/launch`) is asked for before the launch, so the six-second hand-over has a command to show even while the launch holds the single-threaded server.
+
+Three outcomes, all inside the popover so the reader never has to find the board: `launched: true` on a loopback page closes the popover behind the launcher's own toast; `launched: true` on a page that holds a serve key keeps it open with the link, the sentence that the session opened on the machine running `reggie serve`, and the command in the copy field, because the window is on another machine; `launched: false`, a thrown launch, or a launch that runs past six seconds keeps it open with the link, the server's reason (or the "still waiting" sentence), and the command in the copy field, exactly as the board's launcher does through its command field. When even the description cannot be read, the field holds `reggie launch <slug> --run` with `--path` for the page's entity. Choosing a tool from the caret changes the remembered tool for every launch control. A second submit of the same text after an answer is a second item (`<slug>-2`); the server does not deduplicate.
+
+Under the popover the intake gains the line and, for a page with an entity, its last detail line names it (`` Captured from the file `…` ``), which the board card's detail panel and the task page's "What was written" section render with no change to either; the pack the session opens on carries that entity's note chain, the path under "Files in scope", its recent commits and the tasks whose plans overlap it (`docs/ui-api-contract.md`, `POST /api/capture` and `/api/launch`).
